// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Self-test gates. Included only by ontology-qc-selftest.dl, alongside
// control.dl -- the build program must not carry these, or every run warns
// that expected_violation has no facts, and that warning is this repo's most
// reliable signal of a typo'd relation name.
//
// Keying on (check, term) rather than on check name is deliberate. Several
// checks have more than one rule -- obsolete-reference has six -- and a
// name-keyed gate stays satisfied as long as any one arm fires, so a dead arm
// would pass silently.
//
// control.dl therefore plants one term per rule that can go dead on its own.
// That is finer than one per arm: where a single check arm reads a
// vocabulary-layer relation that has several rules of its own, each of those
// gets a term too -- which is why obsolete-reference has six arms but eight
// control terms.
//
// Nothing mechanically forces a NEW arm to be given a term:
// check_without_expectation only fires when a check name has no expectation at
// all, and Datalog gives rules no identity to key on. The convention is the
// available answer.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.decl expected_violation(check:symbol, term:symbol)

// Planted but did not fire: the rule, or one of its arms, is dead.
.decl missing_expected(check:symbol, term:symbol)
missing_expected(c, t) :- expected_violation(c, t), !violation(c, t, _).

// Fired but nobody planted it: the rule has become too broad. This is the
// other direction, and the reason the control ontology can assert that its
// well-formed terms trigger nothing.
.decl unexpected_violation(check:symbol, term:symbol)
unexpected_violation(c, t) :- violation(c, t, _), !expected_violation(c, t).

// Registered, but control.dl plants nothing for it at all.
.decl check_without_expectation(name:symbol)
check_without_expectation(c) :- check(c, _), !expected_violation(c, _).
